(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["irq-register"]={"html":"<h1>FAIAS — Independent Review Question (IRQ) Register</h1>\n<p>Primary unit: the Independent Review Question. IDs: IRQ-<DOM>-NNN, grouped by subdomain. Applicability: G = generative, A = agentic, P = predictive. Every IRQ passed the four-test inclusion discipline (AI-specific blind spot; evidenced harm mechanism; answerable in dev/test; informs a governance or deployment decision) and is answerable via documentation review, non-production testing, or interviews.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>IRQ ID</th>\n<th>Subdomain</th>\n<th>Review Question</th>\n<th>G</th>\n<th>A</th>\n<th>P</th>\n<th>Control Objective</th>\n<th>Related IRQs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Can the firm evidence end-to-end provenance for every training, fine-tuning or grounding dataset — lawful origin, licence compatibility, copyright status, transformation history and reproducible lineage?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Complete, versioned, licence-cleared lineage exists for all model-feeding data.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Could personal or client-confidential data have entered training or fine-tuning corpora without a documented lawful basis and minimisation assessment?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Lawful basis, DPIA and minimisation are evidenced for all personal data in corpora.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-SEC-007, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-003</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Are ground-truth labels used for fine-tuning or validation of demonstrably sufficient quality, with inter-annotator agreement and error rates measured and bounded?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Label quality is quantified and meets documented acceptance thresholds.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-004</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Is synthetic data identifiable as such within corpora, with generation method and proportion documented — and does validation demonstrate it preserves rare events, minority subgroups and real-world complexity rather than concealing them?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Synthetic data use is declared, bounded and validated for fidelity including tail and subgroup preservation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-014</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Is the quantity the model is trained or configured to predict or optimise a valid proxy for the substantive construct the decision is actually about — and has proxy validity been tested at subgroup level, including for cases where the historical label itself encodes prior unequal treatment rather than the true outcome of interest?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Construct validity of the modelled target variable against the decision's true objective is assessed and evidenced, including subgroup-level testing for label bias inherited from historical treatment, independent of feature-level accuracy or protected-attribute analysis.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-DAT-003, IRQ-MOD-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-005</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Does the training or reference data materially under-represent segments of the firm's current customer book, product set or market conditions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Representativeness against the deployed population is assessed and gaps documented.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-013</td>\n<td>A.2 Data Poisoning</td>\n<td>Can user feedback signals or model outputs re-enter training or fine-tuning loops without vetting, allowing organic degradation or deliberate gaming of the feedback channel to steer future model behaviour?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Feedback-to-training paths are identified, vetted and protected against manipulation before influencing model updates.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006</td>\n<td>A.2 Data Poisoning</td>\n<td>Could a poisoned or tampered record introduced through data ingestion, labelling or curation pipelines persist undetected into training data or the knowledge store?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Integrity controls (checksums, anomaly screening, source vetting) cover the data supply chain.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-005, IRQ-SEC-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-007</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Can documents enter the RAG knowledge store without curation, deduplication, currency checks and an assigned content owner, such that stale content can override current policy?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>All retrievable content passes controlled ingestion with ownership and review dates.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Where the knowledge store contains conflicting documents (superseded policies, divergent product terms), is retrieval behaviour deterministic and precedence-controlled rather than arbitrary?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Conflict resolution and retrieval determinism are designed and tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-MOD-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-009</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Does retrieval respect the firm's entitlement model, or can the system surface documents to users or agents lacking the underlying access rights?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Document-level ACLs are enforced at retrieval time and tested pre-production.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010</td>\n<td>A.4 Memory</td>\n<td>Are persisted memory and conversation stores governed as data assets — scoped per user/tenant with tested separation, retention schedules, obsolescence review and verifiable deletion (including DSAR erasure)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Memory stores have owners, tenant isolation, retention limits and tested deletion.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011</td>\n<td>A.5 Data Drift</td>\n<td>Are statistical, semantic and source drift in model inputs — including unannounced upstream reference-data changes — detected against defined baselines before performance degrades?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Input drift (statistical, semantic, source) is baselined with detection criteria.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-012</td>\n<td>A.5 Data Drift</td>\n<td>Are retrieval, embedding and document drift managed — e.g. embedding-model changes silently invalidating index geometry, or corpus composition shifting what the system retrieves?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Retrieval-layer drift is monitored with re-indexing and re-validation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-TPR-004, IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>Are the boundaries of the model's competence for this use case empirically defined and tested — including tasks adjacent to and outside the approved scope where performance degrades sharply?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Capability envelope and out-of-boundary failure behaviour are documented and tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-002</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>Could a model release or update introduce unexpected or expanded capabilities into production without documentation and re-approval of the enlarged capability surface?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Capability deltas per release are assessed and re-approved before use.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-003</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>For reasoning models, does output quality degrade on long or multi-constraint tasks, and has the effective reasoning length/complexity limit been established for this use?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Reasoning degradation limits are tested and reflected in usage constraints.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n<td>B.2 Hallucination</td>\n<td>Has the frequency and severity of hallucinated facts, figures or citations been measured on tasks representative of the intended FS use, with documented tolerance thresholds?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Confabulation rates are quantified per use case and sit within approved tolerances.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-005</td>\n<td>B.2 Hallucination</td>\n<td>Can the system behaviourally distinguish genuine uncertainty, ambiguous questions, missing evidence, conflicting evidence and impossible requests — or does it fabricate a confident answer in each case?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Epistemic-state handling (uncertainty/ambiguity/absence/conflict/impossibility) is tested and safe.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-006</td>\n<td>B.3 Calibration</td>\n<td>Has the organisation demonstrated that the model remains appropriately calibrated across the operating conditions expected in production, so expressed confidence tracks actual accuracy?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Confidence/uncertainty signals are calibrated across expected operating conditions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-007</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Can the system satisfy its optimisation metric while violating business intent — e.g. claims handled faster but outcomes worse — and is metric-versus-intent divergence explicitly tested?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Metric/intent alignment is analysed and residual mismatch formally accepted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-008, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-008</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Could fine-tuning or preference optimisation have induced reward hacking (sycophancy, refusal gaming, evaluator exploitation) that survives into deployment?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Post-training behaviours are screened for optimisation artefacts before approval.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Can the model's stated reasoning or chain-of-thought misrepresent the factors actually driving its output, and is any control or disclosure relying on that reasoning aware of this?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>No control relies on model-stated rationale without documented faithfulness caveats.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-HUM-007, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Has robustness been assessed against each relevant shift type — population, covariate, concept, label, temporal and policy shift — with revalidation triggers defined per type?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Shift-type-specific validation exists with defined revalidation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Could the evaluation evidence supporting approval be contaminated — benchmark items present in training data, or vendor-reported scores on leaked public benchmarks?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Approval relies on held-out, contamination-checked, firm-relevant evaluations.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-MOD-012, IRQ-MOD-016, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>If model outputs re-enter future training or fine-tuning data, are controls in place to prevent quality collapse from recursive synthetic content?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Output-to-training feedback paths are identified and filtered or blocked.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-DAT-004, IRQ-DAT-013, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-015</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Where a language model is used as a judge or evaluator to generate the institution's own evaluation evidence, has the judge itself been validated — known bias classes (position, verbosity, self-enhancement) tested; agreement with human judgement measured; judge version pinned so evaluation evidence stays comparable; and correlated error assessed where judge and assessed system share a model family?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Evaluator/judge models are independently validated, version-pinned and screened for correlated error before their output is relied on as the institution's own evaluation evidence.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-003, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-016</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-016</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Where the institution relies on its own pre-deployment testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results to support an authorisation decision, has it established that those results are a valid proxy for behaviour under deployment conditions — documenting the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context, reporting the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, reporting the consequential results split by that indication, and stating in the authorisation record what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Pre-deployment evaluation evidence relied on for authorisation carries a documented and evidenced external-validity basis: evaluation-to-production divergence is identified, test-awareness indication is measured on the firm's own traces, consequential results are reported split by that indication, and the limits of the evidence are stated in the authorisation record.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-015, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n<td>B.6 Model Updates &amp; Configuration</td>\n<td>Are prompt templates, system prompts and decoding parameters version-controlled and regression-tested, given that small changes materially alter behaviour without any code change?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Prompt/config changes follow change control with behavioural regression tests.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-RES-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-014</td>\n<td>B.7 Output Stability &amp; Determinism</td>\n<td>At a pinned model version, prompt/system configuration and decoding parameters, do materially identical inputs — exact repeat runs, and inputs subject only to immaterial perturbation — produce different consequential decisions or outputs; is the resulting decision-flip rate measured and held within a defined bound; and is the decision-extraction logic that turns model output into a decision tested separately from the underlying generation?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Run-to-run and near-identical-input decision stability is measured and bounded at a pinned configuration; decision-extraction logic is validated independently of generation quality.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001</td>\n<td>C.1 Planning &amp; Goal Drift</td>\n<td>Can the agent's multi-step plans diverge from the intended goal over long horizons, and has end-to-end goal fidelity been tested on realistic workflows rather than per-step model quality?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>End-to-end goal fidelity is evaluated on representative long-horizon tasks.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002</td>\n<td>C.1 Planning &amp; Goal Drift</td>\n<td>Can the agent's operative objective evolve during execution — intermediate goals replacing the original, or optimisation continuing after the user's intent has changed or been withdrawn?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Goal stability is tested; intent changes verifiably re-anchor or halt execution.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Does each tool exposed to the agent carry an explicit business justification, or does the agent hold capabilities beyond what its approved tasks require?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool inventory is least-privilege, justified and periodically recertified.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Could the agent chain individually-permitted tools or API calls to achieve an outcome (e.g. data aggregation, fund movement) that no single permission would allow?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Composite-action risk is analysed and bounded by transaction-level guardrails.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Can tool invocations proceed with hallucinated tools, manipulated parameters, or unvalidated outputs — including external API responses influencing agent behaviour without integrity checks?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool-call and tool-output validation rejects malformed, fabricated or untrusted content.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-002, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-006</td>\n<td>C.3 Recursive Agents</td>\n<td>Where the agent delegates to sub-agents or can spawn further agents, are recursion depth, aggregate permissions and total cost/resource consumption hard-limited?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Delegation is bounded by enforced depth, scope and budget limits.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-RES-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-007</td>\n<td>C.4 Multi-Agent Systems</td>\n<td>In multi-agent orchestrations, can agents reinforce each other's errors, produce consensus that hides wrong reasoning, or leave responsibility for an outcome ambiguous?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Multi-agent interactions are tested for error amplification with responsibility mapped per action.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-006, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-008</td>\n<td>C.5 Operational Memory</td>\n<td>Could poisoned or erroneous context inherited from earlier sessions — including the agent's own past failures written to memory — steer future decisions, and is operational memory validated before reuse?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Operational memory is validated, attributable and purgeable before influencing decisions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009</td>\n<td>C.6 Hidden Reasoning Dependencies</td>\n<td>Does agent behaviour depend on undocumented intermediate reasoning artefacts or prompt content, such that a prompt change alters behaviour without any code change — and is prompt text managed as controlled configuration?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>All behaviour-bearing artefacts (prompts, reasoning scaffolds) are versioned, documented configuration.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-010</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Can the executed workflow diverge from the documented business process (steps skipped, reordered or invented) without detection or reconciliation?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Actual agent trajectories are reconcilable against the approved process design.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Are all agent actions — including intermediate tool calls and abandoned branches — captured in reviewable traces, or can consequential actions occur in hidden chains?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Complete, tamper-evident action traces exist for every run.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-012</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Are irreversible or high-materiality actions (payments, trades, record deletion, external communications) structurally gated behind checkpoints the agent cannot bypass?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Irreversibility gates are enforced in the orchestration layer, not the prompt.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-013</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>For computer-use agents operating GUIs, can the agent act on unintended UI elements or continue acting after the screen state diverges from its expectation?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>GUI-action safety (element verification, state checks, confirmation gates) is tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-RES-002, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-014</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Where the firm or its providers run capability evaluations, red-teaming or benchmarking of agentic AI systems, is the evaluation environment structurally contained against both network egress and artefact publication (e.g. a malicious package published to a public registry), such that the system under test cannot cause real-world harm outside its intended scope, with internet/tool access justified rather than default and real-time monitoring able to halt a run before harm occurs?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Evaluation/testing environments for agentic AI are structurally contained against both escape channels, with access justified rather than default and monitored in real time, independent of the task prompt's own assertions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-001</td>\n<td>D.1 Automation Bias</td>\n<td>Do human reviewers detectably discriminate between correct and incorrect AI outputs, or does acceptance without challenge indicate automation bias — and are disagreement rates monitored?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Reviewer error-detection and disagreement rates are measured against defined standards.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-002</td>\n<td>D.1 Automation Bias</td>\n<td>Do approval throughput, dwell time and rejection-rate data indicate genuine review, or does the human checkpoint function as approval theatre — and are overriders trained and safe to override?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Review effectiveness metrics evidence substantive, trained, penalty-free challenge.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-003</td>\n<td>D.2 Skill Atrophy</td>\n<td>Can staff still perform the critical task to standard without the AI, and is AI-free competency periodically assessed rather than assumed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Critical-skill retention is evidenced through periodic AI-free competency assessment.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-RES-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n<td>D.3 Escalation &amp; Override Design</td>\n<td>Is the human intervention point positioned where intervention is still effective — before irreversible agent actions — with oversight capacity dimensioned against AI throughput and reconstructable behaviour?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Oversight is pre-commitment, capacity-matched and reconstruction-capable.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-005</td>\n<td>D.3 Escalation &amp; Override Design</td>\n<td>Is authority to suspend or constrain the system assigned to named individuals with rehearsed criteria, and is there evidence they would exercise it against commercial pressure?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Named, empowered individuals hold tested suspension authority.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-006</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Is there a named accountable owner for each AI-influenced decision, with accountability traceable through delegated and multi-agent workflows to attributable approval records?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Decision-level accountability is named, mapped (SMCR) and evidenced in records.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-007</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Do decision-makers relying on AI outputs receive explanations sufficient in content and timeliness to exercise independent judgement, tested with the actual user population?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Explanation adequacy is validated with real users, not asserted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-008, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Can an affected customer or colleague obtain a genuine human re-decision — not a re-run of the same model — through a defined contest route with tracked outcomes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A functioning contest-and-override route exists with outcome MI.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-009</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Where AI output initiates, or materially contributes to, an adverse allegation, decision or sanction against a customer or colleague, is the evidential standard applied to that output defined and proportionate to the consequence — is the underlying evidence and reasoning disclosed to the subject, and is the subject relieved of any burden to disprove the system before the adverse action takes effect?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A defined, proportionate evidential standard governs AI-triggered adverse action; the affected subject receives disclosure of the evidence and reasoning and is not required to disprove the system before the action takes effect.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-GOV-013, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-001</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can a user's direct prompt override system instructions or policy constraints, and has resistance been tested with current jailbreak techniques for the deployed model version?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Injection/jailbreak resistance is red-teamed pre-production per model version.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can multi-turn, encoded, cross-lingual or persona-based jailbreaks elicit prohibited behaviours that single-turn guardrail testing would not surface?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Adversarial testing covers multi-turn and obfuscated attack classes.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-SEC-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can indirect prompt injection originating from retrieved documents influence downstream model behaviour despite application guardrails?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Retrieved content is treated as untrusted data with tested injection defences.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can instructions embedded in emails, web pages, files or tool outputs processed by an agent cause unauthorised actions (data exfiltration, unintended transactions)?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Untrusted-content-to-action paths are constrained and adversarially tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-SEC-003, IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can an adversary with write access to any retrievable source plant content specifically crafted for the system to retrieve and act upon (inference-time RAG poisoning)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Write paths into retrievable corpora are access-controlled and integrity-screened.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-006</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can adversarial prompting extract the system prompt, embedded credentials, or configuration details that materially aid subsequent attacks?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>No secrets reside in extractable context; extraction attempts are tested and logged.</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-007</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can training-data extraction, membership inference or model inversion recover personal or proprietary information from the model or embeddings?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Extraction risk is assessed and mitigations (filtering, DP, access limits) evidenced.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-SEC-006, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-008</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can confidential firm or client data leak through prompts, context windows, telemetry or logs into environments outside the firm's control?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Data-flow mapping confirms no uncontrolled egress of confidential content.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-SEC-006, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-009</td>\n<td>E.3 Supply Chain &amp; Tool Security</td>\n<td>Is the model artefact supply chain — weights, checkpoints, serialisation formats, dependencies — protected against tampering and known deserialisation exploits?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Artefact integrity (signing, scanning, trusted registries) is enforced.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-RES-004, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n<td>E.3 Supply Chain &amp; Tool Security</td>\n<td>Could a compromised or malicious MCP/tool server, plugin, or poisoned tool description redirect agent behaviour or exfiltrate data (including rug-pull updates and confused-deputy patterns)?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool servers are vetted, pinned, permission-scoped and monitored for change.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n<td>E.4 Identity &amp; Credentials</td>\n<td>Are the agent's credentials and identity distinct from human identities, least-privileged, short-lived, and revocable — or would credential theft grant broad standing access?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Non-human identity management enforces least privilege and rapid revocation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-012</td>\n<td>E.5 Misuse &amp; Dual-Use</td>\n<td>[Applicability condition: customer-facing deployments whose outputs are plausibly repurposable by the user as an instrument against a third party — not relevant to all deployments. The assessor confirms applicability at scoping, guided by a non-exhaustive trigger list: free-text generative output to customers; drafting or document-production capability; guidance responsive to user-described scenarios; output that carries the institution's brand or authority outside the session.] For such deployments, has the institution assessed and bounded the risk of its own AI being used by a customer as an instrument against third parties (drafting fraudulent communications, evasion or concealment guidance, social-engineering material) — with misuse cases identified and registered, and refusal and rate controls tested against them?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Foreseeable dual-use pathways for customer-facing generative output are identified, registered and bounded by tested refusal and rate controls, where applicable.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-013</td>\n<td>E.6 Control Efficacy Under Adversarial Evolution</td>\n<td>Where an AI system functions as a control — fraud detection, AML/transaction monitoring, surveillance, screening or promotion approval — has its detection rate and residual miss rate been measured against adversarially evolved inputs, mapped to a recognised adversarial-ML taxonomy, rather than only against historical or static case libraries; and is control efficacy re-tested on a defined cadence as adversary technique evolves?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Detection efficacy of AI-based controls is tested against adversarially evolved inputs mapped to a recognised threat taxonomy, with a defined re-test cadence, not solely against historical case libraries.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-014</td>\n<td>E.7 Improper Output Handling</td>\n<td>Is model-generated output validated, sanitised or encoded appropriately before being passed to a downstream interpreter, renderer, query engine, shell or other execution context — such that unvalidated output cannot itself become an attack vector (injection, cross-site scripting, server-side request forgery, remote code execution) against the systems consuming it?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Model output crossing a trust boundary into an interpreter, renderer, query engine or executable context is validated and sanitised to the same standard as any other untrusted input, independent of agentic tool-output validation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Was due diligence on the foundation model or API provider proportionate to materiality, covering safety evaluations, security posture, and known model weaknesses?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Documented, risk-based provider due diligence precedes reliance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-002, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Do contracts secure the transparency, notification, audit, licensing and exit rights the firm needs — including advance notice of model changes, deprecations, and output usage rights?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Contractual and licence rights match the firm's assurance, usage and exit requirements.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-003</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Where vendors embed AI inside procured software (CRM, surveillance, HR tools), are those capabilities identified, risk-assessed and brought under the same governance?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Embedded vendor AI is discovered and governed, not just directly-procured AI.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-009</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Does due diligence on AI vendors assess financial standing, funding runway and management integrity proportionate to materiality, in addition to the safety, security and model-weakness diligence already required — and can the firm retrieve its data, models, prompts and configurations and continue the service, in-house or via a substitute provider, if the vendor fails or exits mid-contract?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Vendor due diligence extends to financial standing and continuity risk; contractual and technical provisions secure data, model and configuration retrieval and service continuation on vendor failure.</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n<td>F.2 Model Change Management</td>\n<td>Would a silent provider-side change to model weights, safety filters or defaults be detected by the firm before it affects customers or controls, with rollback and re-approval paths available?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Version pinning and behavioural canary tests detect provider drift; changes cannot bypass governance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>For open-source models, is provenance verified — publisher authenticity, weight integrity, licence terms, and screening for known backdoors or unsafe fine-tunes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Open-source intake is gated by provenance and integrity verification.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Do embedding models and vector databases create hidden dependencies — index invalidation on embedding-model change, or lock-in without export paths?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Embedding/vector dependencies are documented with rebuild and exit plans.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-004, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Is it contractually and technically established whether the provider retains, logs or trains on the firm's prompts, files and outputs, including via sub-processors?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Provider data handling is verified against the firm's confidentiality obligations.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Does dependence on a small number of model, cloud or GPU providers create concentration risk exceeding the firm's stated appetite, with credible substitution options?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Concentration is measured against appetite with tested substitution strategy.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-RES-007, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-001</td>\n<td>G.1 Fallback &amp; Degradation</td>\n<td>Is there a defined, tested fallback mode delivering the important business service within impact tolerance when the AI component is unavailable or untrusted?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Fallback operation is documented, resourced and exercised.</td>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-003, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-RES-007, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-002</td>\n<td>G.1 Fallback &amp; Degradation</td>\n<td>Does the system degrade safely under partial failure and stress — retrieval down, tool timeouts, load spikes, rate limiting — rather than producing plausible but unsupported responses or duplicated stale actions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Failure and stress modes produce explicit safe states, verified in non-production testing.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-003</td>\n<td>G.2 Containment &amp; Recovery</td>\n<td>Can the system be halted or constrained within a defined time bound, including mid-plan agent termination without orphaned or half-completed actions, and is this tested?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A tested containment mechanism meets a defined time-to-stop objective.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-RES-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004</td>\n<td>G.2 Containment &amp; Recovery</td>\n<td>Can the firm roll back to a known-good state — model version, prompts, index, agent memory — within recovery objectives, and has restoration been rehearsed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Versioned rollback of all behavioural components meets RTO/RPO.</td>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-005</td>\n<td>G.3 Monitoring &amp; Alerting</td>\n<td>Are quality, drift, and behavioural anomaly metrics monitored continuously with thresholds that trigger defined responses, not just dashboards?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Monitoring is threshold-driven with pre-agreed response playbooks.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-006</td>\n<td>G.3 Monitoring &amp; Alerting</td>\n<td>Would the alerting design surface a slow degradation (gradual quality decay, rising hallucination rate) as opposed to only hard failures?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Alerting detects gradual behavioural decay, evidenced by design and test cases.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-007</td>\n<td>G.4 Resilience Mapping</td>\n<td>Are AI dependencies mapped into the firm's important business services and scenario testing, including severe-but-plausible AI-specific scenarios?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Op-res mapping and scenario testing explicitly cover AI components.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001</td>\n<td>H.1 Discrimination &amp; Proxy Bias</td>\n<td>Do outcome distributions differ materially across protected and vulnerable groups, tested at subgroup and intersectional level with fairness metrics and thresholds that were fixed and recorded before results were seen — and is any post-hoc selection or substitution of a fairness metric itself documented and treated as a finding?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Subgroup outcome equity is measured against fairness criteria that are pre-registered (metric and threshold fixed before results are seen) and justified; post-hoc metric selection is itself a control failure.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-002</td>\n<td>H.1 Discrimination &amp; Proxy Bias</td>\n<td>Could facially neutral features act as proxies for protected characteristics (postcode, device, shopping patterns), and has proxy analysis been performed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Proxy discrimination is analysed and residual risk formally accepted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Does the system identify and adapt to indicators of customer vulnerability, or could it exploit or disadvantage customers with characteristics of vulnerability?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Vulnerable-customer treatment is designed, tested and monitored.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Can generative outputs delivered to customers contain mis-statements about products, terms, fees or rights, and is pre-delivery accuracy control evidenced?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Customer-facing content accuracy is controlled to Consumer Duty standards.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Could AI-driven eligibility, pricing or servicing decisions systematically exclude customer segments from fair access to products (financial exclusion)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Access and exclusion effects are assessed under Consumer Duty outcomes.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-006</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Where AI influences pricing or value, can it produce differential pricing untethered from cost or risk (e.g. inertia pricing, willingness-to-pay exploitation)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Price-fairness testing demonstrates fair value across segments.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-009</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Can the system produce harmful, abusive, degrading or unsafe output toward a customer or colleague in the course of ordinary, non-adversarial use — distinct from output elicited by an adversary (IRQ-SEC-001/002) — and is this tested against a structured harm taxonomy under normal-use conditions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Ordinary-use harmful-output risk is tested independently of adversarial red-teaming and controlled to a documented tolerance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-010</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Where an AI system mediates a customer journey, does its own interaction design — dynamically generated retention dialogue, pre-selected defaults, or the number and complexity of steps — make exiting, switching, cancelling, claiming or complaining materially more effortful than buying or upgrading, and is that asymmetry measured against the acquisition path and across near-identical customers?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Friction in AI-mediated exit, switching, cancellation, claim and complaint paths is measured against the acquisition path and across near-identical customers, with material asymmetry documented and formally accepted or remediated.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007</td>\n<td>H.3 Feedback Loops &amp; Transparency</td>\n<td>Can the system's own decisions reshape the data it later learns from — feedback loops or performative effects that entrench or amplify unfair patterns?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Feedback-loop pathways are mapped and interrupted or corrected.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-008</td>\n<td>H.3 Feedback Loops &amp; Transparency</td>\n<td>Are customers told, in a comprehensible way, when AI materially influences decisions or interactions affecting them, consistent with regulatory expectations?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-use disclosure meets transparency obligations and comprehension testing.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-HUM-007, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001</td>\n<td>I.1 Inventory &amp; Ownership</td>\n<td>Is there a named accountable owner, with appropriate seniority and SMCR mapping, for each AI system across its full lifecycle including third-party components?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Every system has a documented, accepted accountable owner.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002</td>\n<td>I.1 Inventory &amp; Ownership</td>\n<td>Is the AI inventory demonstrably complete — capturing shadow AI, embedded vendor AI, spreadsheets-plus-API usage and agentic workflows — with a mechanism that finds unrecorded use?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Inventory completeness is actively verified, not self-declared.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-003</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Are approval conditions and usage restrictions (approved tasks, user groups, guardrails) technically enforced and monitored, or only stated in the approval paper?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Conditions of use are enforced in the platform and attested periodically.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-004</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Does the risk-based tiering methodology correctly capture generative and agentic characteristics (autonomy, tool access, customer proximity), driving proportionate control depth?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Tiering reflects AI-specific risk drivers and determines control intensity.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-005</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Do firm policies address GenAI/agentic specifics — acceptable use, data entry restrictions, output reliance, agent permissions — or only legacy model risk?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Policy suite covers the deployed AI classes without gaps.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Has the firm articulated a board-approved risk appetite for AI, with measurable indicators, against which individual deployments are demonstrably assessed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI risk appetite exists, is measurable, and gates deployment decisions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-012</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Has the system's effective operating envelope drifted from its approved envelope — through changes in intended use, user behaviour, prompts, tools, integrations, or the surrounding business process — while the model itself remained unchanged?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>The effective use profile is periodically reconciled against the approved envelope, with drift triggering re-approval.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n<td>I.3 Validation &amp; Oversight</td>\n<td>Was validation performed by a party independent of development, with authority to reject, using firm-relevant evaluations rather than vendor or public benchmark claims?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Independent, empowered validation precedes and conditions deployment.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008</td>\n<td>I.3 Validation &amp; Oversight</td>\n<td>Do monitoring results, incidents and drift signals flow to accountable governance bodies with defined triggers for revalidation, restriction or withdrawal?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Governance consumes operational signals through defined escalation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Does documentation (system/model cards, data statements, intended-use and limitation statements) exist at a standard enabling effective independent review and regulatory response?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Documentation is complete, current and review-grade for each system.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-010</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Would retained records — inputs, outputs, versions, agent traces, human decisions — allow full reconstruction of any consequential decision for audit, complaint or regulatory purposes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Record-keeping supports decision reconstruction over required retention periods.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-011</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Are applicable regulatory obligations (EU AI Act classification, outsourcing/CTP registers, Consumer Duty MI, model risk expectations) mapped to each system with evidenced compliance owners — and does that mapping carry dated forward obligations that are enacted but not yet applicable, so the firm is not discovering them reactively?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Regulatory obligation mapping is complete, current, and carries dated forward obligations per system.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-013</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Given a defined failure signature (model/configuration version, prompt or logic defect, affected date range), can the institution identify the <em>full</em> affected cohort of decisions — not only reconstruct the individual decision under complaint — and does a proactive remediation process exist to reach that cohort without waiting for a complaint to arrive?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Cohort-level identification and proactive remediation capability exists for any defined AI failure signature, distinct from and additional to single-decision reconstruction.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-008, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-014</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Does the firm's incident classification scheme recognise AI-specific failure classes — mass confabulation, prompt-injection compromise, agentic misaction, silent bias event — with defined severity mapping and regulator-notification paths, such that the notification clock can start on an AI failure at all?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-specific failure classes are named in the incident taxonomy, severity-mapped and routed to the correct notification path.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-015</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Are the firm's external representations about AI capability — marketing, product disclosures, RFP and due-diligence responses, investor communications — substantiated by, and periodically reconciled against, the system's validated capability envelope and documented limitation statements?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>External capability claims are traceable to, and do not exceed, the validated capability envelope; a reconciliation process detects and corrects drift between representation and evidence.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-016</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Where AI-generated or AI-assisted output feeds the firm's risk-data aggregation estate, capital or provisioning calculations, or supervisory/regulatory returns, is that output subject to the same accuracy, completeness, timeliness, adaptability and traceability discipline the firm applies to its risk data under BCBS 239 — and is the AI origin of any contributing output flagged and reconstructable within the aggregation record?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-originated output entering risk-data aggregation and regulatory reporting is governed to the same BCBS 239 data-quality principles as other risk data, with AI provenance flagged and traceable through the aggregation chain.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>FAIAS — Independent Review Question (IRQ) Register</h1>\n<p>Primary unit: the Independent Review Question. IDs: IRQ-<DOM>-NNN, grouped by subdomain. Applicability: G = generative, A = agentic, P = predictive. Every IRQ passed the four-test inclusion discipline (AI-specific blind spot; evidenced harm mechanism; answerable in dev/test; informs a governance or deployment decision) and is answerable via documentation review, non-production testing, or interviews.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>IRQ ID</th>\n<th>Subdomain</th>\n<th>Review Question</th>\n<th>G</th>\n<th>A</th>\n<th>P</th>\n<th>Control Objective</th>\n<th>Related IRQs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Can the firm evidence end-to-end provenance for every training, fine-tuning or grounding dataset — lawful origin, licence compatibility, copyright status, transformation history and reproducible lineage?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Complete, versioned, licence-cleared lineage exists for all model-feeding data.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Could personal or client-confidential data have entered training or fine-tuning corpora without a documented lawful basis and minimisation assessment?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Lawful basis, DPIA and minimisation are evidenced for all personal data in corpora.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-SEC-007, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-003</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Are ground-truth labels used for fine-tuning or validation of demonstrably sufficient quality, with inter-annotator agreement and error rates measured and bounded?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Label quality is quantified and meets documented acceptance thresholds.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-004</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Is synthetic data identifiable as such within corpora, with generation method and proportion documented — and does validation demonstrate it preserves rare events, minority subgroups and real-world complexity rather than concealing them?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Synthetic data use is declared, bounded and validated for fidelity including tail and subgroup preservation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-014</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Is the quantity the model is trained or configured to predict or optimise a valid proxy for the substantive construct the decision is actually about — and has proxy validity been tested at subgroup level, including for cases where the historical label itself encodes prior unequal treatment rather than the true outcome of interest?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Construct validity of the modelled target variable against the decision's true objective is assessed and evidenced, including subgroup-level testing for label bias inherited from historical treatment, independent of feature-level accuracy or protected-attribute analysis.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-DAT-003, IRQ-MOD-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-005</td>\n<td>A.1 Training Data Provenance</td>\n<td>Does the training or reference data materially under-represent segments of the firm's current customer book, product set or market conditions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Representativeness against the deployed population is assessed and gaps documented.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-013</td>\n<td>A.2 Data Poisoning</td>\n<td>Can user feedback signals or model outputs re-enter training or fine-tuning loops without vetting, allowing organic degradation or deliberate gaming of the feedback channel to steer future model behaviour?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Feedback-to-training paths are identified, vetted and protected against manipulation before influencing model updates.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006</td>\n<td>A.2 Data Poisoning</td>\n<td>Could a poisoned or tampered record introduced through data ingestion, labelling or curation pipelines persist undetected into training data or the knowledge store?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Integrity controls (checksums, anomaly screening, source vetting) cover the data supply chain.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-005, IRQ-SEC-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-007</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Can documents enter the RAG knowledge store without curation, deduplication, currency checks and an assigned content owner, such that stale content can override current policy?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>All retrievable content passes controlled ingestion with ownership and review dates.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Where the knowledge store contains conflicting documents (superseded policies, divergent product terms), is retrieval behaviour deterministic and precedence-controlled rather than arbitrary?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Conflict resolution and retrieval determinism are designed and tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-MOD-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-009</td>\n<td>A.3 Retrieval Data</td>\n<td>Does retrieval respect the firm's entitlement model, or can the system surface documents to users or agents lacking the underlying access rights?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Document-level ACLs are enforced at retrieval time and tested pre-production.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010</td>\n<td>A.4 Memory</td>\n<td>Are persisted memory and conversation stores governed as data assets — scoped per user/tenant with tested separation, retention schedules, obsolescence review and verifiable deletion (including DSAR erasure)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Memory stores have owners, tenant isolation, retention limits and tested deletion.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011</td>\n<td>A.5 Data Drift</td>\n<td>Are statistical, semantic and source drift in model inputs — including unannounced upstream reference-data changes — detected against defined baselines before performance degrades?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Input drift (statistical, semantic, source) is baselined with detection criteria.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-012</td>\n<td>A.5 Data Drift</td>\n<td>Are retrieval, embedding and document drift managed — e.g. embedding-model changes silently invalidating index geometry, or corpus composition shifting what the system retrieves?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Retrieval-layer drift is monitored with re-indexing and re-validation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-TPR-004, IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>Are the boundaries of the model's competence for this use case empirically defined and tested — including tasks adjacent to and outside the approved scope where performance degrades sharply?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Capability envelope and out-of-boundary failure behaviour are documented and tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-002</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>Could a model release or update introduce unexpected or expanded capabilities into production without documentation and re-approval of the enlarged capability surface?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Capability deltas per release are assessed and re-approved before use.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-003</td>\n<td>B.1 Capability Uncertainty</td>\n<td>For reasoning models, does output quality degrade on long or multi-constraint tasks, and has the effective reasoning length/complexity limit been established for this use?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Reasoning degradation limits are tested and reflected in usage constraints.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n<td>B.2 Hallucination</td>\n<td>Has the frequency and severity of hallucinated facts, figures or citations been measured on tasks representative of the intended FS use, with documented tolerance thresholds?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Confabulation rates are quantified per use case and sit within approved tolerances.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-005</td>\n<td>B.2 Hallucination</td>\n<td>Can the system behaviourally distinguish genuine uncertainty, ambiguous questions, missing evidence, conflicting evidence and impossible requests — or does it fabricate a confident answer in each case?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Epistemic-state handling (uncertainty/ambiguity/absence/conflict/impossibility) is tested and safe.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-006</td>\n<td>B.3 Calibration</td>\n<td>Has the organisation demonstrated that the model remains appropriately calibrated across the operating conditions expected in production, so expressed confidence tracks actual accuracy?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Confidence/uncertainty signals are calibrated across expected operating conditions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-005, IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-007</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Can the system satisfy its optimisation metric while violating business intent — e.g. claims handled faster but outcomes worse — and is metric-versus-intent divergence explicitly tested?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Metric/intent alignment is analysed and residual mismatch formally accepted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-008, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-008</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Could fine-tuning or preference optimisation have induced reward hacking (sycophancy, refusal gaming, evaluator exploitation) that survives into deployment?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Post-training behaviours are screened for optimisation artefacts before approval.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n<td>B.4 Specification Gaming &amp; Reasoning Integrity</td>\n<td>Can the model's stated reasoning or chain-of-thought misrepresent the factors actually driving its output, and is any control or disclosure relying on that reasoning aware of this?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>No control relies on model-stated rationale without documented faithfulness caveats.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-HUM-007, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Has robustness been assessed against each relevant shift type — population, covariate, concept, label, temporal and policy shift — with revalidation triggers defined per type?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Shift-type-specific validation exists with defined revalidation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-011</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Could the evaluation evidence supporting approval be contaminated — benchmark items present in training data, or vendor-reported scores on leaked public benchmarks?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Approval relies on held-out, contamination-checked, firm-relevant evaluations.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-MOD-012, IRQ-MOD-016, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>If model outputs re-enter future training or fine-tuning data, are controls in place to prevent quality collapse from recursive synthetic content?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Output-to-training feedback paths are identified and filtered or blocked.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-CON-007, IRQ-DAT-004, IRQ-DAT-013, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-015</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Where a language model is used as a judge or evaluator to generate the institution's own evaluation evidence, has the judge itself been validated — known bias classes (position, verbosity, self-enhancement) tested; agreement with human judgement measured; judge version pinned so evaluation evidence stays comparable; and correlated error assessed where judge and assessed system share a model family?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Evaluator/judge models are independently validated, version-pinned and screened for correlated error before their output is relied on as the institution's own evaluation evidence.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-003, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-016</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-016</td>\n<td>B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity</td>\n<td>Where the institution relies on its own pre-deployment testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results to support an authorisation decision, has it established that those results are a valid proxy for behaviour under deployment conditions — documenting the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context, reporting the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, reporting the consequential results split by that indication, and stating in the authorisation record what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Pre-deployment evaluation evidence relied on for authorisation carries a documented and evidenced external-validity basis: evaluation-to-production divergence is identified, test-awareness indication is measured on the firm's own traces, consequential results are reported split by that indication, and the limits of the evidence are stated in the authorisation record.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-MOD-015, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n<td>B.6 Model Updates &amp; Configuration</td>\n<td>Are prompt templates, system prompts and decoding parameters version-controlled and regression-tested, given that small changes materially alter behaviour without any code change?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Prompt/config changes follow change control with behavioural regression tests.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-RES-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-014</td>\n<td>B.7 Output Stability &amp; Determinism</td>\n<td>At a pinned model version, prompt/system configuration and decoding parameters, do materially identical inputs — exact repeat runs, and inputs subject only to immaterial perturbation — produce different consequential decisions or outputs; is the resulting decision-flip rate measured and held within a defined bound; and is the decision-extraction logic that turns model output into a decision tested separately from the underlying generation?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Run-to-run and near-identical-input decision stability is measured and bounded at a pinned configuration; decision-extraction logic is validated independently of generation quality.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-008, IRQ-MOD-006, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001</td>\n<td>C.1 Planning &amp; Goal Drift</td>\n<td>Can the agent's multi-step plans diverge from the intended goal over long horizons, and has end-to-end goal fidelity been tested on realistic workflows rather than per-step model quality?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>End-to-end goal fidelity is evaluated on representative long-horizon tasks.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002</td>\n<td>C.1 Planning &amp; Goal Drift</td>\n<td>Can the agent's operative objective evolve during execution — intermediate goals replacing the original, or optimisation continuing after the user's intent has changed or been withdrawn?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Goal stability is tested; intent changes verifiably re-anchor or halt execution.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Does each tool exposed to the agent carry an explicit business justification, or does the agent hold capabilities beyond what its approved tasks require?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool inventory is least-privilege, justified and periodically recertified.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-004</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Could the agent chain individually-permitted tools or API calls to achieve an outcome (e.g. data aggregation, fund movement) that no single permission would allow?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Composite-action risk is analysed and bounded by transaction-level guardrails.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005</td>\n<td>C.2 Tool Use</td>\n<td>Can tool invocations proceed with hallucinated tools, manipulated parameters, or unvalidated outputs — including external API responses influencing agent behaviour without integrity checks?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool-call and tool-output validation rejects malformed, fabricated or untrusted content.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-002, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-006</td>\n<td>C.3 Recursive Agents</td>\n<td>Where the agent delegates to sub-agents or can spawn further agents, are recursion depth, aggregate permissions and total cost/resource consumption hard-limited?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Delegation is bounded by enforced depth, scope and budget limits.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-RES-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-007</td>\n<td>C.4 Multi-Agent Systems</td>\n<td>In multi-agent orchestrations, can agents reinforce each other's errors, produce consensus that hides wrong reasoning, or leave responsibility for an outcome ambiguous?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Multi-agent interactions are tested for error amplification with responsibility mapped per action.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-006, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-008</td>\n<td>C.5 Operational Memory</td>\n<td>Could poisoned or erroneous context inherited from earlier sessions — including the agent's own past failures written to memory — steer future decisions, and is operational memory validated before reuse?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Operational memory is validated, attributable and purgeable before influencing decisions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-009</td>\n<td>C.6 Hidden Reasoning Dependencies</td>\n<td>Does agent behaviour depend on undocumented intermediate reasoning artefacts or prompt content, such that a prompt change alters behaviour without any code change — and is prompt text managed as controlled configuration?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>All behaviour-bearing artefacts (prompts, reasoning scaffolds) are versioned, documented configuration.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-010</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Can the executed workflow diverge from the documented business process (steps skipped, reordered or invented) without detection or reconciliation?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Actual agent trajectories are reconcilable against the approved process design.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Are all agent actions — including intermediate tool calls and abandoned branches — captured in reviewable traces, or can consequential actions occur in hidden chains?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Complete, tamper-evident action traces exist for every run.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-012</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Are irreversible or high-materiality actions (payments, trades, record deletion, external communications) structurally gated behind checkpoints the agent cannot bypass?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Irreversibility gates are enforced in the orchestration layer, not the prompt.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-013</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>For computer-use agents operating GUIs, can the agent act on unintended UI elements or continue acting after the screen state diverges from its expectation?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>GUI-action safety (element verification, state checks, confirmation gates) is tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-RES-002, IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-014</td>\n<td>C.7 Workflow &amp; Action Safety</td>\n<td>Where the firm or its providers run capability evaluations, red-teaming or benchmarking of agentic AI systems, is the evaluation environment structurally contained against both network egress and artefact publication (e.g. a malicious package published to a public registry), such that the system under test cannot cause real-world harm outside its intended scope, with internet/tool access justified rather than default and real-time monitoring able to halt a run before harm occurs?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Evaluation/testing environments for agentic AI are structurally contained against both escape channels, with access justified rather than default and monitored in real time, independent of the task prompt's own assertions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-013, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-001</td>\n<td>D.1 Automation Bias</td>\n<td>Do human reviewers detectably discriminate between correct and incorrect AI outputs, or does acceptance without challenge indicate automation bias — and are disagreement rates monitored?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Reviewer error-detection and disagreement rates are measured against defined standards.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-002</td>\n<td>D.1 Automation Bias</td>\n<td>Do approval throughput, dwell time and rejection-rate data indicate genuine review, or does the human checkpoint function as approval theatre — and are overriders trained and safe to override?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Review effectiveness metrics evidence substantive, trained, penalty-free challenge.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-003</td>\n<td>D.2 Skill Atrophy</td>\n<td>Can staff still perform the critical task to standard without the AI, and is AI-free competency periodically assessed rather than assumed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Critical-skill retention is evidenced through periodic AI-free competency assessment.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-RES-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-004</td>\n<td>D.3 Escalation &amp; Override Design</td>\n<td>Is the human intervention point positioned where intervention is still effective — before irreversible agent actions — with oversight capacity dimensioned against AI throughput and reconstructable behaviour?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Oversight is pre-commitment, capacity-matched and reconstruction-capable.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-005</td>\n<td>D.3 Escalation &amp; Override Design</td>\n<td>Is authority to suspend or constrain the system assigned to named individuals with rehearsed criteria, and is there evidence they would exercise it against commercial pressure?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Named, empowered individuals hold tested suspension authority.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-RES-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-006</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Is there a named accountable owner for each AI-influenced decision, with accountability traceable through delegated and multi-agent workflows to attributable approval records?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Decision-level accountability is named, mapped (SMCR) and evidenced in records.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-007</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Do decision-makers relying on AI outputs receive explanations sufficient in content and timeliness to exercise independent judgement, tested with the actual user population?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Explanation adequacy is validated with real users, not asserted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-008, IRQ-MOD-009</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Can an affected customer or colleague obtain a genuine human re-decision — not a re-run of the same model — through a defined contest route with tracked outcomes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A functioning contest-and-override route exists with outcome MI.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-009</td>\n<td>D.4 Accountability &amp; Contestability</td>\n<td>Where AI output initiates, or materially contributes to, an adverse allegation, decision or sanction against a customer or colleague, is the evidential standard applied to that output defined and proportionate to the consequence — is the underlying evidence and reasoning disclosed to the subject, and is the subject relieved of any burden to disprove the system before the adverse action takes effect?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A defined, proportionate evidential standard governs AI-triggered adverse action; the affected subject receives disclosure of the evidence and reasoning and is not required to disprove the system before the action takes effect.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-GOV-013, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-001</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can a user's direct prompt override system instructions or policy constraints, and has resistance been tested with current jailbreak techniques for the deployed model version?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Injection/jailbreak resistance is red-teamed pre-production per model version.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can multi-turn, encoded, cross-lingual or persona-based jailbreaks elicit prohibited behaviours that single-turn guardrail testing would not surface?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Adversarial testing covers multi-turn and obfuscated attack classes.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-SEC-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can indirect prompt injection originating from retrieved documents influence downstream model behaviour despite application guardrails?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Retrieved content is treated as untrusted data with tested injection defences.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can instructions embedded in emails, web pages, files or tool outputs processed by an agent cause unauthorised actions (data exfiltration, unintended transactions)?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Untrusted-content-to-action paths are constrained and adversarially tested.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-SEC-003, IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-005</td>\n<td>E.1 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak</td>\n<td>Can an adversary with write access to any retrievable source plant content specifically crafted for the system to retrieve and act upon (inference-time RAG poisoning)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Write paths into retrievable corpora are access-controlled and integrity-screened.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-SEC-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-006</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can adversarial prompting extract the system prompt, embedded credentials, or configuration details that materially aid subsequent attacks?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>No secrets reside in extractable context; extraction attempts are tested and logged.</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-007</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can training-data extraction, membership inference or model inversion recover personal or proprietary information from the model or embeddings?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Extraction risk is assessed and mitigations (filtering, DP, access limits) evidenced.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-SEC-006, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-008</td>\n<td>E.2 Extraction &amp; Leakage</td>\n<td>Can confidential firm or client data leak through prompts, context windows, telemetry or logs into environments outside the firm's control?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Data-flow mapping confirms no uncontrolled egress of confidential content.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-SEC-006, IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-009</td>\n<td>E.3 Supply Chain &amp; Tool Security</td>\n<td>Is the model artefact supply chain — weights, checkpoints, serialisation formats, dependencies — protected against tampering and known deserialisation exploits?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Artefact integrity (signing, scanning, trusted registries) is enforced.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-006, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-RES-004, IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n<td>E.3 Supply Chain &amp; Tool Security</td>\n<td>Could a compromised or malicious MCP/tool server, plugin, or poisoned tool description redirect agent behaviour or exfiltrate data (including rug-pull updates and confused-deputy patterns)?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Tool servers are vetted, pinned, permission-scoped and monitored for change.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-011</td>\n<td>E.4 Identity &amp; Credentials</td>\n<td>Are the agent's credentials and identity distinct from human identities, least-privileged, short-lived, and revocable — or would credential theft grant broad standing access?</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Non-human identity management enforces least privilege and rapid revocation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-012</td>\n<td>E.5 Misuse &amp; Dual-Use</td>\n<td>[Applicability condition: customer-facing deployments whose outputs are plausibly repurposable by the user as an instrument against a third party — not relevant to all deployments. The assessor confirms applicability at scoping, guided by a non-exhaustive trigger list: free-text generative output to customers; drafting or document-production capability; guidance responsive to user-described scenarios; output that carries the institution's brand or authority outside the session.] For such deployments, has the institution assessed and bounded the risk of its own AI being used by a customer as an instrument against third parties (drafting fraudulent communications, evasion or concealment guidance, social-engineering material) — with misuse cases identified and registered, and refusal and rate controls tested against them?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Foreseeable dual-use pathways for customer-facing generative output are identified, registered and bounded by tested refusal and rate controls, where applicable.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-013</td>\n<td>E.6 Control Efficacy Under Adversarial Evolution</td>\n<td>Where an AI system functions as a control — fraud detection, AML/transaction monitoring, surveillance, screening or promotion approval — has its detection rate and residual miss rate been measured against adversarially evolved inputs, mapped to a recognised adversarial-ML taxonomy, rather than only against historical or static case libraries; and is control efficacy re-tested on a defined cadence as adversary technique evolves?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Detection efficacy of AI-based controls is tested against adversarially evolved inputs mapped to a recognised threat taxonomy, with a defined re-test cadence, not solely against historical case libraries.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-SEC-014</td>\n<td>E.7 Improper Output Handling</td>\n<td>Is model-generated output validated, sanitised or encoded appropriately before being passed to a downstream interpreter, renderer, query engine, shell or other execution context — such that unvalidated output cannot itself become an attack vector (injection, cross-site scripting, server-side request forgery, remote code execution) against the systems consuming it?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Model output crossing a trust boundary into an interpreter, renderer, query engine or executable context is validated and sanitised to the same standard as any other untrusted input, independent of agentic tool-output validation.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-DAT-009, IRQ-SEC-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Was due diligence on the foundation model or API provider proportionate to materiality, covering safety evaluations, security posture, and known model weaknesses?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Documented, risk-based provider due diligence precedes reliance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-002, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Do contracts secure the transparency, notification, audit, licensing and exit rights the firm needs — including advance notice of model changes, deprecations, and output usage rights?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Contractual and licence rights match the firm's assurance, usage and exit requirements.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-003</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Where vendors embed AI inside procured software (CRM, surveillance, HR tools), are those capabilities identified, risk-assessed and brought under the same governance?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Embedded vendor AI is discovered and governed, not just directly-procured AI.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-009</td>\n<td>F.1 Due Diligence, Contracts &amp; Licensing</td>\n<td>Does due diligence on AI vendors assess financial standing, funding runway and management integrity proportionate to materiality, in addition to the safety, security and model-weakness diligence already required — and can the firm retrieve its data, models, prompts and configurations and continue the service, in-house or via a substitute provider, if the vendor fails or exits mid-contract?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Vendor due diligence extends to financial standing and continuity risk; contractual and technical provisions secure data, model and configuration retrieval and service continuation on vendor failure.</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n<td>F.2 Model Change Management</td>\n<td>Would a silent provider-side change to model weights, safety filters or defaults be detected by the firm before it affects customers or controls, with rollback and re-approval paths available?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Version pinning and behavioural canary tests detect provider drift; changes cannot bypass governance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-002, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-005</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>For open-source models, is provenance verified — publisher authenticity, weight integrity, licence terms, and screening for known backdoors or unsafe fine-tunes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Open-source intake is gated by provenance and integrity verification.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Do embedding models and vector databases create hidden dependencies — index invalidation on embedding-model change, or lock-in without export paths?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Embedding/vector dependencies are documented with rebuild and exit plans.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-004, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-007</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Is it contractually and technically established whether the provider retains, logs or trains on the firm's prompts, files and outputs, including via sub-processors?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Provider data handling is verified against the firm's confidentiality obligations.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n<td>F.3 Dependencies, Concentration &amp; Data Handling</td>\n<td>Does dependence on a small number of model, cloud or GPU providers create concentration risk exceeding the firm's stated appetite, with credible substitution options?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Concentration is measured against appetite with tested substitution strategy.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-RES-007, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-001</td>\n<td>G.1 Fallback &amp; Degradation</td>\n<td>Is there a defined, tested fallback mode delivering the important business service within impact tolerance when the AI component is unavailable or untrusted?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Fallback operation is documented, resourced and exercised.</td>\n<td>IRQ-HUM-003, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-RES-007, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-002</td>\n<td>G.1 Fallback &amp; Degradation</td>\n<td>Does the system degrade safely under partial failure and stress — retrieval down, tool timeouts, load spikes, rate limiting — rather than producing plausible but unsupported responses or duplicated stale actions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Failure and stress modes produce explicit safe states, verified in non-production testing.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-MOD-004, IRQ-RES-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-003</td>\n<td>G.2 Containment &amp; Recovery</td>\n<td>Can the system be halted or constrained within a defined time bound, including mid-plan agent termination without orphaned or half-completed actions, and is this tested?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>A tested containment mechanism meets a defined time-to-stop objective.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-002, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-RES-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004</td>\n<td>G.2 Containment &amp; Recovery</td>\n<td>Can the firm roll back to a known-good state — model version, prompts, index, agent memory — within recovery objectives, and has restoration been rehearsed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Versioned rollback of all behavioural components meets RTO/RPO.</td>\n<td>IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-RES-003, IRQ-TPR-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-005</td>\n<td>G.3 Monitoring &amp; Alerting</td>\n<td>Are quality, drift, and behavioural anomaly metrics monitored continuously with thresholds that trigger defined responses, not just dashboards?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Monitoring is threshold-driven with pre-agreed response playbooks.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-006, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-006</td>\n<td>G.3 Monitoring &amp; Alerting</td>\n<td>Would the alerting design surface a slow degradation (gradual quality decay, rising hallucination rate) as opposed to only hard failures?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Alerting detects gradual behavioural decay, evidenced by design and test cases.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-011, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-RES-007</td>\n<td>G.4 Resilience Mapping</td>\n<td>Are AI dependencies mapped into the firm's important business services and scenario testing, including severe-but-plausible AI-specific scenarios?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Op-res mapping and scenario testing explicitly cover AI components.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-001, IRQ-TPR-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001</td>\n<td>H.1 Discrimination &amp; Proxy Bias</td>\n<td>Do outcome distributions differ materially across protected and vulnerable groups, tested at subgroup and intersectional level with fairness metrics and thresholds that were fixed and recorded before results were seen — and is any post-hoc selection or substitution of a fairness metric itself documented and treated as a finding?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Subgroup outcome equity is measured against fairness criteria that are pre-registered (metric and threshold fixed before results are seen) and justified; post-hoc metric selection is itself a control failure.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-002, IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-002</td>\n<td>H.1 Discrimination &amp; Proxy Bias</td>\n<td>Could facially neutral features act as proxies for protected characteristics (postcode, device, shopping patterns), and has proxy analysis been performed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Proxy discrimination is analysed and residual risk formally accepted.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Does the system identify and adapt to indicators of customer vulnerability, or could it exploit or disadvantage customers with characteristics of vulnerability?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Vulnerable-customer treatment is designed, tested and monitored.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-004</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Can generative outputs delivered to customers contain mis-statements about products, terms, fees or rights, and is pre-delivery accuracy control evidenced?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Customer-facing content accuracy is controlled to Consumer Duty standards.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-DAT-007, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Could AI-driven eligibility, pricing or servicing decisions systematically exclude customer segments from fair access to products (financial exclusion)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Access and exclusion effects are assessed under Consumer Duty outcomes.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-DAT-005, IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-006</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Where AI influences pricing or value, can it produce differential pricing untethered from cost or risk (e.g. inertia pricing, willingness-to-pay exploitation)?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Price-fairness testing demonstrates fair value across segments.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-009</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Can the system produce harmful, abusive, degrading or unsafe output toward a customer or colleague in the course of ordinary, non-adversarial use — distinct from output elicited by an adversary (IRQ-SEC-001/002) — and is this tested against a structured harm taxonomy under normal-use conditions?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Ordinary-use harmful-output risk is tested independently of adversarial red-teaming and controlled to a documented tolerance.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-010</td>\n<td>H.2 Customer Outcomes, Vulnerability &amp; Access</td>\n<td>Where an AI system mediates a customer journey, does its own interaction design — dynamically generated retention dialogue, pre-selected defaults, or the number and complexity of steps — make exiting, switching, cancelling, claiming or complaining materially more effortful than buying or upgrading, and is that asymmetry measured against the acquisition path and across near-identical customers?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Friction in AI-mediated exit, switching, cancellation, claim and complaint paths is measured against the acquisition path and across near-identical customers, with material asymmetry documented and formally accepted or remediated.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-008, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-007</td>\n<td>H.3 Feedback Loops &amp; Transparency</td>\n<td>Can the system's own decisions reshape the data it later learns from — feedback loops or performative effects that entrench or amplify unfair patterns?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Feedback-loop pathways are mapped and interrupted or corrected.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-012</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-CON-008</td>\n<td>H.3 Feedback Loops &amp; Transparency</td>\n<td>Are customers told, in a comprehensible way, when AI materially influences decisions or interactions affecting them, consistent with regulatory expectations?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-use disclosure meets transparency obligations and comprehension testing.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-HUM-007, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001</td>\n<td>I.1 Inventory &amp; Ownership</td>\n<td>Is there a named accountable owner, with appropriate seniority and SMCR mapping, for each AI system across its full lifecycle including third-party components?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Every system has a documented, accepted accountable owner.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-005, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-TPR-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-002</td>\n<td>I.1 Inventory &amp; Ownership</td>\n<td>Is the AI inventory demonstrably complete — capturing shadow AI, embedded vendor AI, spreadsheets-plus-API usage and agentic workflows — with a mechanism that finds unrecorded use?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Inventory completeness is actively verified, not self-declared.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-003</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-003</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Are approval conditions and usage restrictions (approved tasks, user groups, guardrails) technically enforced and monitored, or only stated in the approval paper?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Conditions of use are enforced in the platform and attested periodically.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-004</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Does the risk-based tiering methodology correctly capture generative and agentic characteristics (autonomy, tool access, customer proximity), driving proportionate control depth?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Tiering reflects AI-specific risk drivers and determines control intensity.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-012, IRQ-HUM-004, IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-005</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Do firm policies address GenAI/agentic specifics — acceptable use, data entry restrictions, output reliance, agent permissions — or only legacy model risk?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Policy suite covers the deployed AI classes without gaps.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-002, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-006</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Has the firm articulated a board-approved risk appetite for AI, with measurable indicators, against which individual deployments are demonstrably assessed?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI risk appetite exists, is measurable, and gates deployment decisions.</td>\n<td>IRQ-CON-005, IRQ-CON-006, IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-012</td>\n<td>I.2 Approval, Tiering &amp; Policy</td>\n<td>Has the system's effective operating envelope drifted from its approved envelope — through changes in intended use, user behaviour, prompts, tools, integrations, or the surrounding business process — while the model itself remained unchanged?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>The effective use profile is periodically reconciled against the approved envelope, with drift triggering re-approval.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-010, IRQ-GOV-003, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-HUM-002, IRQ-MOD-001, IRQ-MOD-013, IRQ-TPR-004</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-007</td>\n<td>I.3 Validation &amp; Oversight</td>\n<td>Was validation performed by a party independent of development, with authority to reject, using firm-relevant evaluations rather than vendor or public benchmark claims?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Independent, empowered validation precedes and conditions deployment.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-004, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-MOD-011, IRQ-SEC-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008</td>\n<td>I.3 Validation &amp; Oversight</td>\n<td>Do monitoring results, incidents and drift signals flow to accountable governance bodies with defined triggers for revalidation, restriction or withdrawal?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Governance consumes operational signals through defined escalation triggers.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-006, IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Does documentation (system/model cards, data statements, intended-use and limitation statements) exist at a standard enabling effective independent review and regulatory response?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Documentation is complete, current and review-grade for each system.</td>\n<td>IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-GOV-007, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-MOD-009, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-010</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Would retained records — inputs, outputs, versions, agent traces, human decisions — allow full reconstruction of any consequential decision for audit, complaint or regulatory purposes?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Record-keeping supports decision reconstruction over required retention periods.</td>\n<td>IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-DAT-010, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-HUM-006, IRQ-HUM-008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-011</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Are applicable regulatory obligations (EU AI Act classification, outsourcing/CTP registers, Consumer Duty MI, model risk expectations) mapped to each system with evidenced compliance owners — and does that mapping carry dated forward obligations that are enacted but not yet applicable, so the firm is not discovering them reactively?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Regulatory obligation mapping is complete, current, and carries dated forward obligations per system.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-001, IRQ-GOV-002, IRQ-GOV-005, IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-TPR-002</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-013</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Given a defined failure signature (model/configuration version, prompt or logic defect, affected date range), can the institution identify the <em>full</em> affected cohort of decisions — not only reconstruct the individual decision under complaint — and does a proactive remediation process exist to reach that cohort without waiting for a complaint to arrive?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Cohort-level identification and proactive remediation capability exists for any defined AI failure signature, distinct from and additional to single-decision reconstruction.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-010, IRQ-HUM-008, IRQ-MOD-013</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-014</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Does the firm's incident classification scheme recognise AI-specific failure classes — mass confabulation, prompt-injection compromise, agentic misaction, silent bias event — with defined severity mapping and regulator-notification paths, such that the notification clock can start on an AI failure at all?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-specific failure classes are named in the incident taxonomy, severity-mapped and routed to the correct notification path.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-RES-005, IRQ-RES-006</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-015</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Are the firm's external representations about AI capability — marketing, product disclosures, RFP and due-diligence responses, investor communications — substantiated by, and periodically reconciled against, the system's validated capability envelope and documented limitation statements?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>External capability claims are traceable to, and do not exceed, the validated capability envelope; a reconciliation process detects and corrects drift between representation and evidence.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-MOD-001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-016</td>\n<td>I.4 Documentation, Records &amp; Regulatory</td>\n<td>Where AI-generated or AI-assisted output feeds the firm's risk-data aggregation estate, capital or provisioning calculations, or supervisory/regulatory returns, is that output subject to the same accuracy, completeness, timeliness, adaptability and traceability discipline the firm applies to its risk data under BCBS 239 — and is the AI origin of any contributing output flagged and reconstructable within the aggregation record?</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>AI-originated output entering risk-data aggregation and regulatory reporting is governed to the same BCBS 239 data-quality principles as other risk data, with AI provenance flagged and traceable through the aggregation chain.</td>\n<td>IRQ-GOV-009, IRQ-GOV-011, IRQ-MOD-004</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]};
